now in the race for space travel a  manned rocket has taken a very special  tourist into orbit 90 year old actor  william shatner best known for playing  captain kirk in the cold science fiction  series star trek after blasting off the  crew travels for roughly 11 minutes and  experienced a few minutes of  weightlessness it was the second manned  rocket by blue origin one of the  companies in the race to commercialize  space travel  william shatner was one of four crew  members and is now the oldest person to  travel into orbit after he landed he was  greeted by blue origin founder jeff  bezos  mr shatner was visibly moved by the  experience  i'm so  filled with emotion about what just  happened i i just  it's extraordinary  i hope i never recover from it the  vulnerability  of everything it's so small  this air which is keeping us alive  is thinner than your skin  it's it's a it's a  it's a sliver it's  it's immeasurably small well keith  cowing is editor of the website space  spaceref.com welcome to dw um is sending  captain kirk into space anything more  than just a great marketing by jeff  bezos  it is great marketing and if you want to  get the perfect pitch man you want one  that's perfectly aligned with your  product and if you know of a better one  please let me know i think this was  flawless in terms of marketing  and what sort of demands is a 11 minute  flight into orbit likely to put on a 90  year old like william shatner  well i actually did some training for a  blue uh for a virgin galactic flight in  the centrifuge which is a little more  rigorous and i was in my late 50s and i  pulled six two point g's and i was you  know just fine afterwards so what this  shows is that virtually anybody who can  walk up and down the stairs has the  right stuff and can go into space  so long as they can afford to buy the  ticket or get a free one william yeah i  saw him interviewed and he was saying  that the toughest thing about the whole  thing was actually getting out of the  chair  yeah  well imagine that it's easier to go into  space than to you know get in and out of  a chair that should that should tell you  something  who benefits from this besides the the  money man does space tourism benefit  science or or is it just about the money  well you know it depends which subset of  this if it's just getting in for a quick  ride then you know maybe it has limited  benefit but then again you know you go  on vacation or holidays that has clear  benefit for people but the more  important thing is that these vehicles  that can go up and come back and be  reused have a potential to do more than  just going into space or going through  space shall we say spacex is building a  large starship which is much bigger  which will allow what's called a  suborbital hop in other words you get in  one of these rockets in london and  you're in sydney australia 50 minutes  later so the potential for these is not  what they do today but what they can  lead to in the next five to ten years  isn't that interesting and so  looking perhaps  10 years perhaps 50 years can we see can  you see  the idea of space being commercialized  can you see people i don't know take  paying to go to the moon for instance  and then actually staying up there  uh i i can imagine it of course i'm  predisposed to do that because i've been  imagining it since i was a little little  boy but uh yeah i think it's become real  in the sense that  if this follows the path of other things  like air travel it started as something  that only rich people could do and now  we complain about you know not having  enough leg room so you know if people if  enough people want to do something there  are people who will find a way to offer  it to people for cheap  okay carrying uh thank you so much  you've given me much to think about  there thank you  my pleasure
 

